<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rajadhiraja Chola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1054">1054</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Rajadhiraja I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rajadhiraja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> was a Chola king, the eldest son of Rajendra I, who was co-ruler from 1018 and sole ruler from 1044 to 1054. A warlike king, he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and repeatedly against the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, and claimed to have sacked their capital Kalyani. He was killed in 1054 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-koppam">Battle of Koppam</a> while fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-i">Someshvara I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rajadhiraja-chola.jpg" alt="Rajadhiraja Chola" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Rajadhiraja_Chola.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the Thiruvilandur copper plates, his birth name was Jayangondachola. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruvalangadu-copper-plates">Tiruvalangadu copper plates</a> of Rajendra I record that he was made Chola-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> viceroy over the Pandya country around 1018, and his inscriptions show him exercising royal authority and campaigning alongside his father. One reading of the Thiruvilandur inscription holds that Rajendra I may have meant to make his younger son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-ii">Rajendra II</a> heir shortly before his death, but that Rajendra II declined in favour of his elder brother.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, where Sinhalese resistance had grown since Rajendra I deported King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahinda-v">Mahinda V</a> in 1017, the rebel king Vikramabahu attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> armies with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> prince Vikrama Pandya and Jagatpala, a prince from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannauj">Kanyakubja</a>; Rajadhiraja's forces killed both princes. The Buddhist chronicle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a> describes the treatment the Cholas gave their enemies as harsh, and historians note that it was written from a Buddhist point of view.</p><p>Against the Western Chalukyas he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-i">Someshvara I</a> at Pundur and drove his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> and Vijayaditya north beyond the Tungabhadra, burning Kollippakkai. In 1046 he led an expedition into the Telugu country and won at Dannada on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, and his armies later demolished the Chalukya palace at Kampili and set up a victory pillar at Yadgir. According to his inscriptions he then entered the Chalukya capital at Kalyanapura, was anointed there as Vijayarajendra, and carried off a stone door guardian inscribed as brought after burning Kalyanapuram. An inscription of his 29th year records his performance of the horse sacrifice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Someshvara stopped paying tribute and intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vengi">Vengi</a>, Rajadhiraja made his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajendra-chola-ii">Rajendra II</a> co-regent in preference to his own sons and invaded the southern Chalukya lands. At Koppam on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> he fought from his elephant and was killed by a shower of arrows; Rajendra II rallied the army and Someshvara withdrew. He was remembered as Yanai-mel-thunjina Devar, the king who died on the elephant. A Western Chalukya inscription at Annigere calls him a wicked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> who burned Jaina temples. Like his father he was a Shaiva, and a record at Tiruvorriyur names the sixty-three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rajadhiraja I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Someshvara I renewed the contest and at the Battle of Koppam in 1054 the Chola king <b>Rajadhiraja I</b> was killed, although his brother Rajendra II rallied the army and both sides claimed victory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
